Carry out aggregation and transposing.

#Abundance tibble of samples (rows) against genera (columns)
genus_tibble  &lt;- pseq |&gt;
    #Aggregate to genus
    microbiome::aggregate_taxa("Genus", verbose = FALSE) |&gt;
    #Extract otu_table
    phyloseq::otu_table() |&gt;
    #Convert to data.frame and transpose
    as.data.frame() |&gt; t() |&gt;
    #Convert to tibble
    dplyr::as_tibble(rownames = "Sample")
#Display top 6 rows of tibble
head(genus_tibble)

You may notice that we do not use the function microbiome::otu_tibble() in the above code. This is because transposing a tibble will convert our tibble into a transposed matrix rather than a transposed tibble. This matrix has our column names in the first row which we do not want. Therefore, it is easier to go from phyloseq -&gt; otu_table -&gt; data.frame -&gt; transposed -&gt; tibble. Annoyingly complex but
If you want to see this in action you can type and run the below demonstrative code.

#Demonstration on why we don't use microibome::otu_tibble()
pseq |&gt;
    #Aggregate to genus
    microbiome::aggregate_taxa("Genus", verbose = FALSE) |&gt;
    #Extract otu_table as a tibble
    microbiome::otu_tibble() |&gt;
    #Transpose
    t() |&gt;
    #View top 6 rows
    head()
